WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.215
Lesson 1.9.

00:00:02.215 --> 00:00:04.760
Smart accounts and account abstraction.

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:07.219
By the end, you'll understand how a smart

00:00:07.219 --> 00:00:10.029
account changes wallet security, and how to use

00:00:10.029 --> 00:00:12.195
its features without adding new risk.

00:00:13.495 --> 00:00:14.785
Here's the plan.

00:00:14.785 --> 00:00:16.535
What actually separates a normal

00:00:16.535 --> 00:00:18.320
wallet from a smart account.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:22.074
E.R.C. forty-three thirty-seven, the standard that makes

00:00:22.074 --> 00:00:25.930
smart accounts work, without changing Ethereum itself.

00:00:25.930 --> 00:00:28.525
The real features that standard unlocks.

00:00:28.525 --> 00:00:30.590
And the new risks worth knowing before

00:00:30.590 --> 00:00:31.755
you rely on any of it.

00:00:33.055 --> 00:00:34.220
Two kinds of wallet.

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:38.345
Here's the actual difference, side by side.

00:00:38.345 --> 00:00:41.848
An E.O.A., an externally owned account, is a normal

00:00:41.848 --> 00:00:46.055
wallet: one private key controls it completely, full stop.

00:00:46.055 --> 00:00:49.079
A smart account is a contract wallet instead:

00:00:49.079 --> 00:00:53.220
its rules, multisig, limits, recovery, whatever you configure,

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:55.915
are code you choose, not a fixed default.

00:00:57.215 --> 00:00:59.170
Worth being precise about what this

00:00:59.170 --> 00:01:01.480
doesn't change, before going further.

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:04.324
A smart account doesn't make you invulnerable, and it

00:01:04.324 --> 00:01:07.310
doesn't replace good judgement about what you sign.

00:01:07.310 --> 00:01:10.087
It changes what's structurally possible for your

00:01:10.087 --> 00:01:11.900
wallet to do, and what a single

00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:14.240
point of failure can actually reach.

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:17.160
Everything else from this module still applies, in full.

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:19.550
Quick check.

00:01:19.550 --> 00:01:22.061
What's the core structural difference between

00:01:22.061 --> 00:01:23.535
an EOA and a smart account?

00:01:27.955 --> 00:01:30.231
The answer: an EOA is controlled

00:01:30.231 --> 00:01:32.325
by one private key, fixed.

00:01:32.325 --> 00:01:35.432
A smart account is a contract wallet whose rules,

00:01:35.432 --> 00:01:38.975
like multisig or spending limits, you configure yourself.

00:01:40.275 --> 00:01:42.715
ERC-4337.

00:01:44.015 --> 00:01:47.427
Here's exactly how a smart-account transaction actually

00:01:47.427 --> 00:01:49.925
gets processed, step by step, since it's

00:01:49.925 --> 00:01:52.540
genuinely different from a normal one.

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:54.438
You sign what's called a “user

00:01:54.438 --> 00:01:57.125
operation”, not a standard transaction.

00:01:57.125 --> 00:01:59.322
A network of bundlers collects these

00:01:59.322 --> 00:02:02.175
user operations and packages them together.

00:02:02.175 --> 00:02:05.183
A paymaster can optionally step in and cover

00:02:05.183 --> 00:02:08.057
the gas, in a token like U.S.D.C., instead

00:02:08.057 --> 00:02:11.165
of you needing the network's native asset.

00:02:11.165 --> 00:02:14.635
And the bundle finally executes on-chain, as one batch.

00:02:15.935 --> 00:02:18.591
Worth being precise on why this standard

00:02:18.591 --> 00:02:21.701
exists at all, since “without changing Ethereum

00:02:21.701 --> 00:02:23.945
itself” is the important part.

00:02:23.945 --> 00:02:27.119
Earlier attempts at account abstraction needed a

00:02:27.119 --> 00:02:30.098
change to Ethereum's own core protocol, which

00:02:30.098 --> 00:02:32.730
is slow and difficult to coordinate.

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:37.984
E.R.C. forty-three thirty-seven achieves the same result entirely through

00:02:37.984 --> 00:02:42.031
smart contracts and this bundler network, layered on top

00:02:42.031 --> 00:02:44.800
of Ethereum exactly as it already runs.

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:48.940
One real number, so this isn't theoretical.

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:53.221
Industry trackers have recorded tens of millions

00:02:53.221 --> 00:02:57.239
of ERC-4337 smart accounts deployed since the

00:02:57.239 --> 00:03:01.820
standard launched in 2023, across major networks.

00:03:01.820 --> 00:03:05.008
This is outside research, not this program's own

00:03:05.008 --> 00:03:08.392
data, but it's real, current infrastructure, not an

00:03:08.392 --> 00:03:11.385
experimental idea still waiting to be adopted.

00:03:12.685 --> 00:03:13.775
Quick check.

00:03:13.775 --> 00:03:16.260
What does a paymaster actually do in this system?

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:23.046
The answer: it can pay the gas for your

00:03:23.046 --> 00:03:26.358
transaction on your behalf, for example letting you pay

00:03:26.358 --> 00:03:29.375
fees in USDC instead of the network's native token.

00:03:30.675 --> 00:03:31.600
The features.

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:34.356
Here are the five features

00:03:34.356 --> 00:03:36.435
that actually matter in practice.

00:03:36.435 --> 00:03:40.158
Multisig and social recovery: trusted “guardians” can

00:03:40.158 --> 00:03:42.940
restore access if you lose a device.

00:03:42.940 --> 00:03:45.563
Spending limits: a daily cap that holds

00:03:45.563 --> 00:03:47.895
even if one key is compromised.

00:03:47.895 --> 00:03:50.825
Session keys: a limited, temporary key for one

00:03:50.825 --> 00:03:54.305
app, covered properly in Lesson fourteen point two.

00:03:54.305 --> 00:03:57.368
Batching: several actions, like an approval and

00:03:57.368 --> 00:04:00.285
a swap, combined into a single signature.

00:04:00.285 --> 00:04:03.156
And passkeys: logging in with your device's

00:04:03.156 --> 00:04:05.832
own biometrics, instead of a seed phrase.

00:04:07.132 --> 00:04:10.180
Zoom into social recovery specifically, since it's

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:12.512
the feature people ask about most.

00:04:12.512 --> 00:04:14.508
You choose a set of guardians in

00:04:14.508 --> 00:04:17.047
advance, people or devices you trust.

00:04:17.047 --> 00:04:19.092
If you lose access, a guardian, or

00:04:19.092 --> 00:04:22.137
enough of them together, can initiate recovery.

00:04:22.137 --> 00:04:24.274
That recovery then waits out a delay

00:04:24.274 --> 00:04:27.173
you set yourself, commonly measured in hours.

00:04:27.173 --> 00:04:30.017
And during that entire delay, you can cancel it

00:04:30.017 --> 00:04:33.158
yourself, instantly, if it wasn't you who started it.

00:04:34.458 --> 00:04:36.968
And here's session keys, step by step,

00:04:36.968 --> 00:04:39.542
since “a limited, temporary key for one

00:04:39.542 --> 00:04:42.673
app” undersells how useful this actually is.

00:04:42.673 --> 00:04:44.752
You grant one specific app a session

00:04:44.752 --> 00:04:46.738
key, not your main key at all.

00:04:46.738 --> 00:04:50.032
That key is scoped narrowly: maybe only this game,

00:04:50.032 --> 00:04:53.303
only this contract, only a small spending cap.

00:04:53.303 --> 00:04:55.256
You use the app freely, without a

00:04:55.256 --> 00:04:57.738
signing prompt on every single action.

00:04:57.738 --> 00:05:00.307
And the session key simply expires, on its

00:05:00.307 --> 00:05:02.578
own, on a schedule you set in advance.

00:05:03.877 --> 00:05:06.892
And passkeys deserve their own contrast against

00:05:06.892 --> 00:05:10.207
everything Module One taught about seed phrases.

00:05:10.207 --> 00:05:12.201
A seed phrase is words you must

00:05:12.201 --> 00:05:14.757
write down, store, and never lose, ever,

00:05:14.757 --> 00:05:17.737
with catastrophic consequences if it leaks.

00:05:17.737 --> 00:05:21.520
A passkey uses your device's own biometrics, fingerprint or

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:24.798
face, to authorise a signature; there's no words to

00:05:24.798 --> 00:05:27.699
write down, and no phrase that can be phished

00:05:27.699 --> 00:05:29.527
out of you over a phone call.

00:05:30.827 --> 00:05:33.236
And here's exactly why batching matters more

00:05:33.236 --> 00:05:35.142
than it sounds like it should.

00:05:35.142 --> 00:05:37.888
Without it, approving a token and then swapping

00:05:37.888 --> 00:05:40.920
it are two separate signatures, two separate moments

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:43.737
something could go wrong or get interrupted.

00:05:43.737 --> 00:05:47.112
Batched, through a smart account, they're one signature: you

00:05:47.112 --> 00:05:50.432
either approve-and-swap together, or nothing happens at all.

00:05:51.732 --> 00:05:52.822
Quick check.

00:05:52.822 --> 00:05:55.526
What can guardians do in social recovery, and

00:05:55.526 --> 00:05:58.172
what protects you if a recovery wasn't yours?

00:06:02.592 --> 00:06:05.331
The answer: guardians can restore your access

00:06:05.331 --> 00:06:06.997
if you lose it, and a delay

00:06:06.997 --> 00:06:09.141
you set yourself gives you a window

00:06:09.141 --> 00:06:11.522
to cancel any recovery you didn't start.

00:06:12.822 --> 00:06:13.667
New risks.

00:06:14.967 --> 00:06:17.325
Here's exactly what to check that an

00:06:17.325 --> 00:06:20.002
EOA never required, since a smart account

00:06:20.002 --> 00:06:22.342
genuinely adds new surface area.

00:06:22.342 --> 00:06:25.734
Read the account's actual contract code, or at least

00:06:25.734 --> 00:06:30.577
confirm it's a known, audited implementation, not something unverified.

00:06:30.577 --> 00:06:33.854
Review every module or plugin's permissions the same

00:06:33.854 --> 00:06:36.512
way you'd review a token approval, since a

00:06:36.512 --> 00:06:39.532
plugin can hold real power over the account.

00:06:39.532 --> 00:06:42.664
Think through guardian collusion specifically: could two

00:06:42.664 --> 00:06:45.877
guardians you chose plausibly coordinate against you.

00:06:45.877 --> 00:06:49.563
And check whether your smart-account address is actually deployed

00:06:49.563 --> 00:06:51.517
on every chain you plan to send to.

00:06:52.817 --> 00:06:55.919
Here's what actually auditing a plugin's permissions

00:06:55.919 --> 00:06:58.912
looks like, concretely, not just in principle.

00:06:58.912 --> 00:07:01.089
Find out exactly what the plugin can

00:07:01.089 --> 00:07:04.213
do: which functions, which assets, which limits.

00:07:04.213 --> 00:07:07.036
Check whether it can be upgraded later without your

00:07:07.036 --> 00:07:10.973
explicit signature, since that changes what you're really trusting.

00:07:10.973 --> 00:07:13.124
Confirm it's from a known, audited

00:07:13.124 --> 00:07:15.453
source, not an unverified add-on.

00:07:15.453 --> 00:07:17.375
And remove any plugin the moment

00:07:17.375 --> 00:07:18.948
you stop actively using it.

00:07:20.248 --> 00:07:23.085
One specific trap worth naming on its own, since

00:07:23.085 --> 00:07:26.453
it catches people who otherwise did everything right.

00:07:26.453 --> 00:07:30.078
The same smart-account address doesn't necessarily exist,

00:07:30.078 --> 00:07:32.503
or hold any funds, on every chain.

00:07:32.503 --> 00:07:34.866
Sending to that address on a chain where

00:07:34.866 --> 00:07:37.633
your smart account was never deployed can leave

00:07:37.633 --> 00:07:40.804
funds sitting somewhere you genuinely can't reach, not

00:07:40.804 --> 00:07:43.398
lost to an attacker, just structurally stuck.

00:07:44.698 --> 00:07:47.727
Put guardian collusion risk next to the alternative,

00:07:47.727 --> 00:07:50.998
since “trust your guardians” isn't the whole answer.

00:07:50.998 --> 00:07:53.949
Poorly chosen guardians, say, three close friends

00:07:53.949 --> 00:07:56.723
who all talk daily, could plausibly coordinate

00:07:56.723 --> 00:07:59.738
and recover your account without your consent.

00:07:59.738 --> 00:08:03.524
Well-chosen guardians, people or entities with no reason or

00:08:03.524 --> 00:08:07.563
means to collude, genuinely can't produce that outcome together.

00:08:08.863 --> 00:08:09.868
Worked example.

00:08:11.168 --> 00:08:13.293
Here's a real configuration.

00:08:13.293 --> 00:08:15.350
Your operating wallet becomes a smart

00:08:15.350 --> 00:08:17.978
account with three things layered together.

00:08:17.978 --> 00:08:20.435
A passkey on your phone plus a hardware

00:08:20.435 --> 00:08:23.928
key, required together (2-of-2), for any large move.

00:08:23.928 --> 00:08:26.564
A $2,000/day limit for small moves,

00:08:26.564 --> 00:08:28.988
using just the passkey alone.

00:08:28.988 --> 00:08:32.974
And 3 guardians (2-of-3) who can recover access, but

00:08:32.974 --> 00:08:36.133
only after a 48-hour delay you can cancel.

00:08:37.433 --> 00:08:39.184
Now play out the two scenarios

00:08:39.184 --> 00:08:41.348
this setup is actually built for.

00:08:41.348 --> 00:08:43.918
Lose your phone, and you recover through your

00:08:43.918 --> 00:08:47.068
three guardians, waiting out the cancellable delay.

00:08:47.068 --> 00:08:49.698
Have your phone stolen instead, and the thief

00:08:49.698 --> 00:08:52.042
is capped at two thousand dollars a day,

00:08:52.042 --> 00:08:54.615
with forty-eight hours for you to cancel any

00:08:54.615 --> 00:08:57.073
recovery attempt you didn't start yourself.

00:08:58.373 --> 00:08:59.833
Here's your checklist.

00:08:59.833 --> 00:09:01.368
Do it now, for real.

00:09:01.368 --> 00:09:03.505
You know exactly which of your wallets

00:09:03.505 --> 00:09:05.943
are EOAs, and which are smart accounts.

00:09:05.943 --> 00:09:08.161
Your guardians are chosen so that no

00:09:08.161 --> 00:09:10.620
two of them could plausibly collude, and

00:09:10.620 --> 00:09:13.018
recovery always has a cancel delay.

00:09:13.018 --> 00:09:15.716
Module and plugin permissions get reviewed the

00:09:15.716 --> 00:09:17.968
same way you'd review an approval.

00:09:17.968 --> 00:09:21.072
And your smart account is deployed, or deployable,

00:09:21.072 --> 00:09:23.263
on every chain you actually send to.

00:09:24.563 --> 00:09:25.808
Let's recap.

00:09:25.808 --> 00:09:27.991
An EOA is one fixed key; a smart

00:09:27.991 --> 00:09:31.268
account is a contract whose rules you choose.

00:09:31.268 --> 00:09:36.754
E.R.C. forty-three thirty-seven makes that possible without touching Ethereum's

00:09:36.754 --> 00:09:41.168
own core protocol, through bundlers and optional paymasters.

00:09:41.168 --> 00:09:45.508
The real features, guardians, spending limits, session keys, batching

00:09:45.508 --> 00:09:49.838
and passkeys, are genuinely useful, when configured deliberately.

00:09:49.838 --> 00:09:52.303
And the new risks, contract code, plugin

00:09:52.303 --> 00:09:55.791
permissions, guardian collusion and chain deployment, are

00:09:55.791 --> 00:09:58.978
exactly what deliberate configuration means checking.

00:10:00.278 --> 00:10:04.243
Choose your guardians deliberately, review plugin permissions like

00:10:04.243 --> 00:10:07.557
approvals, and check your smart account actually exists

00:10:07.557 --> 00:10:09.573
on every chain you use it on.

00:10:09.573 --> 00:10:12.198
Next up, Module 2: DeFi mechanics.
